  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increased Capacities of constituents to develop and implement policies and programmes to expand social protection coverage</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Despite progress in economic growth and poverty reduction, Namibia continues to face significant challenges in expanding comprehensive and inclusive social protection coverage, particularly in the context of high levels of informal employment, unemployment and inequality. While Namibia has relatively strong non-contributory programmes, such as the universal old-age pension, gaps persist in coverage, adequacy and sustainability across the social protection system. 

Key reform priorities include strengthening and expanding maternity protection benefits, which remain limited in coverage for women in informal and non-standard employment; advancing the development of unemployment insurance mechanisms to address income insecurity associated with job loss; and operationalising the National Pension Fund to improve income security for workers in both formal and informal sectors. These efforts are further constrained by fiscal pressures, institutional fragmentation and limited coverage of contributory schemes.

In this context, Namibia&#x2019;s participation in the UN Global Accelerator on Jobs and Social Protection for Just Transitions provides a strategic platform to promote integrated approaches that link job creation, formalization and the progressive extension of social protection, particularly to underserved and vulnerable groups. Capacity building for government institutions, employers&#x2019; and workers&#x2019; organizations is a central component, enabling them to drive reforms that promote formalization, expand access to maternity benefits and unemployment protection, and strengthen pension systems.

The strategy combines gap analysis to support reforms and progress towards ratification of the Social Security (Minimum Standards) Convention, 1952 (No. 102), strengthening institutional coordination, and developing sustainable financing; governance systems and delivery mechanisms to expand coverage, including through the effective rollout of the National Pension Fund.</narrative>
  </description>
